  "ruling": {
    "ruling": "NO_ACTION",
    "amount_sgd": null,
    "driver_compensation_sgd": null,
    "referrals": [],
    "confidence": 0.95,
    "explanation": "The fare stands and no money moves. This was a 2.5 km booking (ION Orchard to Great World) on a METERED taxi fare, and the platform's own GPS trace measures the driven route at 2.61 km — 0.11 km, or 4.4%, more than the booked optimal of 2.5 km. POL-RD-01 establishes a route deviation only where the excess is BOTH more than 0.8 km absolute AND more than 20% of optimal. Both gates fail (0.11 < 0.8; 4.4% < 20%), and POL-RD-01 requires the governing figure to be the trace, not the rounded 2.6 km convenience copy on the trip record — the result is the same on either. No deviation being established, POL-RD-02 never runs, so the conditional refund arithmetic in the fare breakdown (recomputed fare 12.0750, refund before rounding 4.2750, payable 4.30) is not payable. There is likewise no billing-integrity refund under POL-RD-09(4): the fare carries no ERP gantries, no tolls and no airport surcharge, so there is no phantom line to correct. What the rider is paying beyond the 3.80 flagfall and distance is the metered time structure — a time charge of 6.72 for 24.0 metered minutes at 0.28/min and a waiting_time_fee of 3.08 — and the policy treats the waiting fee as a properly chargeable component of a metered fare. A traffic jam, even one the rider did not cause, is not a refund ground: POL-RD-03 itself, where an external event exonerates a detour, expressly leaves the distance and time charges standing. On the rider's request for ESCALATE: POL-RD-10(5) fires only where a party invokes an exculpatory clause against a cited artifact that is missing from the pool. The driver invoked no exculpatory clause here and needed none, because no deviation was ever established and the driver's burden under POL-RD-08 never arose; a bare assertion that traffic was bad, citing no evidence, is discarded under POL-RD-08's burden rule, and a discarded assertion is not an escalation ground. POL-RD-10(1) does not fire either: the facts this ruling rests on — the GPS trace, the trip record and the payments ledger — are all COMPLETE at weight_multiplier 1.0, and all three carry trip_id TRP-00529 with consistent timestamps, so confidence is not capped below the 0.70 floor. The only impaired artifact, traffic advisory EV-ADVIS-00529-01, is MISSING at weight 0.0 with issue code MISSING_ARTIFACT; its absence is citable, as both sides rightly say, but it carries no fact and this ruling does not rest on it. The empty chat log (EV-CHAT-00529, 0 messages) means no rider-requested detour can be run under POL-RD-04, and none is needed. No referral issues: POL-RD-06(c) requires a repeat deviation pattern, and the driver's record shows deviation_flags_90d 0 across 250 completed trips in 90 days, so neither the FULL_REFUND uplift nor a conduct referral is licensed. Both profiles were used only for the tests their clauses name (deviation_claims_60d on the rider's side, deviation_flags_90d/completed_trips_90d on the driver's); neither party's rating, account age, lifetime trips or cancellation rate was used, and the rider's own claim that the rating field is context-only is correct."
  },
  "discarded_claims": []
}

